1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,175
This is my mosquito spray.

2
00:00:02,287 --> 00:00:05,150
I took it to a tiny, warm
island in the Caribbean,

3
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:06,762
packed solid with houses.

4
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:08,288
The seal's still on.

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:09,913
Nothing there wanted to bite me.

6
00:00:10,025 --> 00:00:11,462
Hi, I'm Wob.

7
00:00:11,575 --> 00:00:14,425
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

8
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:21,813
The island is Santa Cruz del Islote,
off Colombia's Caribbean coast.

9
00:00:21,925 --> 00:00:25,462
From the boat, it looked like someone
had parked a neighbourhood on the sea.

10
00:00:25,575 --> 00:00:28,625
It covers about as much
ground as two soccer fields.

11
00:00:28,738 --> 00:00:33,163
Newspapers often call it one of the most
densely populated islands in the world.

12
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:35,125
No one agrees on the headcount, though.

13
00:00:35,238 --> 00:00:37,663
Around it sit bigger
islands, with beaches,

14
00:00:37,713 --> 00:00:39,238
trees and room to spare.

15
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,012
So why squeeze onto this one?

16
00:00:41,125 --> 00:00:42,788
The answer was very small.

17
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:46,138
The clue rode in my side
pocket the whole way,

18
00:00:46,188 --> 00:00:47,575
and I still guessed wrong.

19
00:00:48,675 --> 00:00:50,900
I came to see if there
was room for one more.

20
00:00:51,012 --> 00:00:52,800
I'd packed a huge backpack.

21
00:00:52,912 --> 00:00:54,588
That was my first mistake.

22
00:00:56,204 --> 00:00:59,214
The boat from Cartagena
takes a couple of hours,

23
00:00:59,284 --> 00:01:00,534
more if the sea is rough.

24
00:01:00,824 --> 00:01:03,954
I spent them holding the backpack
on my lap like a nervous dog.

25
00:01:04,224 --> 00:01:05,574
We both got splashed.

26
00:01:05,904 --> 00:01:07,524
There's a shorter ride from Tolú,

27
00:01:07,574 --> 00:01:10,754
forty-five minutes to an
hour by small motorboat,

28
00:01:10,904 --> 00:01:12,234
depending on who you ask.

29
00:01:12,524 --> 00:01:14,034
I found that out on the way back.

30
00:01:14,384 --> 00:01:15,184
Up close,

31
00:01:15,244 --> 00:01:16,954
the island does have land under it.

32
00:01:17,084 --> 00:01:17,974
Just not much.

33
00:01:18,344 --> 00:01:20,534
More than a hundred houses
stand shoulder to shoulder.

34
00:01:20,584 --> 00:01:24,574
Colombia's twenty eighteen census
counted a hundred and twenty.

35
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:27,594
There's a school, a restaurant
and a little square.

36
00:01:27,904 --> 00:01:31,494
In the square stands a cross, and
the cross gave the island its name.

37
00:01:31,724 --> 00:01:32,614
There are no cars.

38
00:01:33,024 --> 00:01:36,254
A car here would run out of island
long before it ran out of fuel.

39
00:01:36,584 --> 00:01:38,534
I looked for somewhere to put my bag down.

40
00:01:38,824 --> 00:01:39,674
I'm still looking.

41
00:01:41,184 --> 00:01:43,759
So I walked around the
whole island before lunch.

42
00:01:43,872 --> 00:01:45,722
I finished before I got hungry.

43
00:01:45,834 --> 00:01:48,496
Then I tried a shortcut
between a pair of houses.

44
00:01:48,609 --> 00:01:50,184
My backpack tried it too.

45
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:51,922
Only one of us made it through.

46
00:01:52,034 --> 00:01:52,834
I pulled.

47
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:54,522
The backpack pulled back.

48
00:01:54,634 --> 00:01:56,434
The houses didn't move at all.

49
00:01:56,547 --> 00:01:59,859
A kid strolled past, looked
at me, looked at the backpack,

50
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:02,909
and kept going, with the kind
of politeness that hurts.

51
00:02:03,022 --> 00:02:06,122
I took it off and carried it
in front of me like a sofa.

52
00:02:06,234 --> 00:02:10,246
Every corner I turned, the backpack
turned it first and hit something.

53
00:02:10,359 --> 00:02:13,634
A cat on a windowsill watched
the whole thing without blinking.

54
00:02:13,747 --> 00:02:15,659
Island rule: bring less.

55
00:02:15,772 --> 00:02:17,059
Then bring less again.

56
00:02:17,171 --> 00:02:20,171
By lunchtime, the backpack
and I weren't speaking.

57
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,075
So why here, of all the islands?

58
00:02:24,188 --> 00:02:25,300
I had a theory.

59
00:02:25,412 --> 00:02:26,212
Treasure.

60
00:02:26,275 --> 00:02:30,388
A tiny island, a long time ago,
somebody must have buried something.

61
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,263
I borrowed a plastic spade.

62
00:02:32,375 --> 00:02:34,325
The ground was coral and cement.

63
00:02:34,437 --> 00:02:36,275
The spade folded like a napkin.

64
00:02:36,388 --> 00:02:38,250
I dug with my hands for a while.

65
00:02:38,362 --> 00:02:41,338
I found one lost button and
a strong sense of shame.

66
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:43,800
Then I asked around
about the island's past,

67
00:02:43,850 --> 00:02:46,038
and the real story beat treasure easily.

68
00:02:46,150 --> 00:02:49,975
About a hundred and fifty years ago,
this was a bare little island with nobody

69
00:02:50,025 --> 00:02:50,887
living on it.

70
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,800
Fishermen working these waters used
it to rest and to wait out storms.

71
00:02:54,912 --> 00:02:56,238
That explains a stop.

72
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:57,900
It doesn't explain a town.

73
00:02:59,116 --> 00:03:01,704
The town part comes down
to something very small.

74
00:03:01,816 --> 00:03:05,091
Night fell, the fishermen
settled in, and nothing bit them.

75
00:03:05,204 --> 00:03:07,954
The islands next door have
mangroves and beaches,

76
00:03:08,004 --> 00:03:10,028
and that's where the
biting crowd hangs out.

77
00:03:10,141 --> 00:03:14,166
Locals say this island had neither,
so the mosquitoes stayed over there.

78
00:03:14,278 --> 00:03:17,853
Picture a party where everyone's
invited except the guest who bites.

79
00:03:17,966 --> 00:03:19,566
You'd stay at that party too.

80
00:03:19,679 --> 00:03:21,116
So the fishermen stayed.

81
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:23,966
And since the island was
small, they made it bigger.

82
00:03:24,078 --> 00:03:26,654
They piled up coral, stone and shells,

83
00:03:26,704 --> 00:03:29,954
plus whatever the sea washed up,
and later they poured cement.

84
00:03:30,066 --> 00:03:33,704
Some of the ground under me was built
by somebody's great-grandparents.

85
00:03:33,816 --> 00:03:34,916
I tried to help.

86
00:03:35,029 --> 00:03:39,616
I found a nice flat shell, carried it to
the edge and set it down very carefully.

87
00:03:39,728 --> 00:03:41,916
The island didn't get noticeably bigger.

88
00:03:42,029 --> 00:03:42,891
Fair enough.

89
00:03:44,308 --> 00:03:46,333
I didn't take the story on trust.

90
00:03:46,445 --> 00:03:50,383
That evening I sat on the edge of the
island and held one arm out over the water,

91
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:51,233
like bait.

92
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:55,220
Across the channel, I could practically
hear the mosquitoes planning dinner.

93
00:03:55,333 --> 00:03:56,158
Over here,

94
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:57,170
nothing.

95
00:03:57,033 --> 00:03:59,408
I waved the arm a little, to look tastier.

96
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:00,496
Still nothing.

97
00:04:00,608 --> 00:04:03,421
I took my hat off, in case
the hat was the problem.

98
00:04:03,533 --> 00:04:04,333
It wasn't.

99
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:07,233
At midnight I went to bed
with the spray in my hand,

100
00:04:07,333 --> 00:04:09,871
unopened, like a sword
I never got to swing.

101
00:04:09,983 --> 00:04:11,621
In the morning I checked my arm.

102
00:04:11,733 --> 00:04:12,620
Not a bump.

103
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:14,996
I have never been so
offended to be ignored.

104
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:19,058
Next, an easy question, I thought.

105
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:20,633
How many people live here?

106
00:04:20,745 --> 00:04:23,545
A figure you'll see everywhere
is about twelve hundred.

107
00:04:23,657 --> 00:04:27,983
A man who'd lived here his whole life
told CNN it was closer to nine hundred.

108
00:04:28,095 --> 00:04:32,632
Colombia's national census in twenty
eighteen counted a little over four hundred.

109
00:04:32,745 --> 00:04:34,995
The one before, in two thousand five,

110
00:04:35,045 --> 00:04:36,757
counted five hundred and eighty.

111
00:04:36,870 --> 00:04:38,932
Estimates vary, to put it gently.

112
00:04:39,045 --> 00:04:41,370
Think of a jar of jelly beans at a fair.

113
00:04:41,483 --> 00:04:46,132
Everyone guesses, nobody agrees, and
the beans keep hopping on and off boats.

114
00:04:46,245 --> 00:04:47,945
I bought a clicker counter anyway.

115
00:04:48,058 --> 00:04:49,132
It overheated.

116
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:52,535
I gave up counting and just watched.

117
00:04:52,648 --> 00:04:55,298
Whatever the real number
is, the island is full.

118
00:04:55,410 --> 00:04:56,622
Doors stand open.

119
00:04:56,735 --> 00:04:59,710
Neighbours chat across the lane
without raising their voices,

120
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:02,135
because the lane is
narrower than my backpack.

121
00:05:02,248 --> 00:05:04,722
Kids dart between walls that almost touch.

122
00:05:04,835 --> 00:05:08,935
A foundation and the Colombian Navy
repainted the fronts of the houses,

123
00:05:08,985 --> 00:05:11,698
and nine murals went up,
painted with the community.

124
00:05:11,810 --> 00:05:13,960
In the evening I sat in the little square,

125
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:18,110
next to the cross, and the whole island
seemed to stroll past at least once.

126
00:05:18,222 --> 00:05:19,998
Somebody's radio was playing.

127
00:05:20,110 --> 00:05:22,210
Somebody's dinner
smelled better than mine.

128
00:05:22,322 --> 00:05:26,385
I ate my crackers very slowly, to
make them last as long as the smell.

129
00:05:26,497 --> 00:05:28,235
And my spray was still sealed.

130
00:05:28,347 --> 00:05:29,647
I changed my mind.

131
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:31,110
Small might be fine.

132
00:05:31,222 --> 00:05:32,572
Small might be great.

133
00:05:34,292 --> 00:05:36,705
The next day, I kept track of the boats.

134
00:05:36,817 --> 00:05:39,942
The earliest leaves before
sunrise, full of nets.

135
00:05:40,054 --> 00:05:42,930
Fishing and tourism keep a
lot of families here going.

136
00:05:43,042 --> 00:05:46,242
By some accounts, the tourists
are catching up with the fish.

137
00:05:46,354 --> 00:05:47,930
The next boat was my problem.

138
00:05:48,042 --> 00:05:51,267
I wanted a shower, and there's
no fresh water on the island.

139
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:54,529
No river, no spring, just coral and salt.

140
00:05:54,642 --> 00:05:58,442
The Colombian Navy has brought drinking
water to these islands by boat.

141
00:05:58,555 --> 00:06:02,567
A bank even donated a machine that pulls
drinking water out of the humid air.

142
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:04,054
It sounds like magic.

143
00:06:04,167 --> 00:06:08,355
It's condensation, the same trick as
a cold glass sweating on a hot day.

144
00:06:08,467 --> 00:06:12,592
Colombia's housing ministry has also
looked at solar-powered machines that turn

145
00:06:12,642 --> 00:06:14,529
seawater into drinking water.

146
00:06:14,642 --> 00:06:16,680
For now, I showered with a cup.

147
00:06:17,892 --> 00:06:20,154
In the afternoon I went
looking for a socket.

148
00:06:20,267 --> 00:06:24,592
In twenty twenty-one, Colombia's energy
ministry upgraded the island's solar and

149
00:06:24,642 --> 00:06:29,404
diesel power system, with a new diesel
generator and a tune-up for its batteries.

150
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:30,904
I plugged in my phone.

151
00:06:31,017 --> 00:06:32,829
The batteries took it personally.

152
00:06:32,942 --> 00:06:36,304
My phone charged a little, and
I decided a little was plenty.

153
00:06:36,417 --> 00:06:40,655
Later, speedboats bring day visitors
from hotels on nearby islands,

154
00:06:40,705 --> 00:06:42,717
and tourism matters more every year.

155
00:06:42,830 --> 00:06:45,629
Some islanders reportedly
work at those hotels.

156
00:06:45,742 --> 00:06:48,592
I joined a group of visitors
for a lap of the island.

157
00:06:48,705 --> 00:06:51,767
We finished before anyone's
ice cream had a chance to melt.

158
00:06:51,879 --> 00:06:56,017
Then the fishing boats came home, and the
whole day folded back into the island.

159
00:06:56,129 --> 00:06:57,717
Every hour had a boat in it.

160
00:06:57,830 --> 00:07:00,455
Nobody here ended up on
this island by accident.

161
00:07:02,184 --> 00:07:05,309
On my last night I strung a hammock
near the edge of the island.

162
00:07:05,421 --> 00:07:07,972
I wanted to fall asleep
to the sound of the waves.

163
00:07:08,084 --> 00:07:08,884
It worked.

164
00:07:08,984 --> 00:07:11,146
I fell asleep to the sound of the waves.

165
00:07:11,259 --> 00:07:12,696
I woke up to the feel of them.

166
00:07:12,809 --> 00:07:16,584
The sea had come up in the night and
was standing politely under my hammock,

167
00:07:16,672 --> 00:07:18,334
like a guest who turned up early.

168
00:07:18,447 --> 00:07:19,784
I pulled my legs in.

169
00:07:19,896 --> 00:07:21,134
I pulled my hat in.

170
00:07:21,247 --> 00:07:23,096
I pulled in everything I could reach.

171
00:07:23,209 --> 00:07:26,447
My backpack, on the floor,
didn't have that option.

172
00:07:26,559 --> 00:07:29,609
It drifted off down the lane like
it had somewhere better to be.

173
00:07:31,832 --> 00:07:34,607
Back home, I set the backpack on my desk.

174
00:07:34,719 --> 00:07:35,807
It's still drying.

175
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:38,832
For the people out there, that
night isn't a funny story.

176
00:07:38,945 --> 00:07:43,269
The Guardian reported that islanders sometimes
wake up to find water in their homes.

177
00:07:43,382 --> 00:07:47,019
These islands sit only about two
metres above the sea on average.

178
00:07:47,132 --> 00:07:49,232
That's roughly six and a half feet.

179
00:07:49,345 --> 00:07:52,845
A local report in twenty
fourteen, cited by The Guardian,

180
00:07:52,957 --> 00:07:57,157
found erosion along about forty-five
percent of the archipelago's coastline.

181
00:07:57,270 --> 00:08:00,132
One small island nearby
has disappeared completely.

182
00:08:00,244 --> 00:08:05,094
Global sea levels are expected to rise by
up to thirty centimetres by twenty fifty.

183
00:08:05,207 --> 00:08:06,382
That's about a foot.

184
00:08:06,495 --> 00:08:08,682
Imagine a table standing in a bathtub,

185
00:08:08,732 --> 00:08:10,120
with the tap left running.

186
00:08:10,232 --> 00:08:12,194
A hammock story is funny once.

187
00:08:12,307 --> 00:08:15,282
Living with it, year after
year, is something else.

188
00:08:15,395 --> 00:08:16,944
I changed my mind back.

189
00:08:19,168 --> 00:08:21,568
So why not pack up and
move somewhere drier?

190
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,493
I wondered about that on the boat out,

191
00:08:23,543 --> 00:08:27,218
while the island shrank behind me
into a handful of roofs on the water.

192
00:08:27,330 --> 00:08:29,406
I drew its boat routes in my notebook.

193
00:08:29,518 --> 00:08:31,793
One line ran out to the fishing grounds,

194
00:08:31,843 --> 00:08:33,805
where the nets go before the sun is up.

195
00:08:33,918 --> 00:08:36,243
Another ran next door, to the hotels.

196
00:08:36,356 --> 00:08:38,981
A third ran toward the
mainland, for school.

197
00:08:39,093 --> 00:08:42,893
Young people who want to keep studying
past the island's school have to leave.

198
00:08:43,006 --> 00:08:46,806
I tried to picture leaving a place where
everyone I love fits inside one evening

199
00:08:46,856 --> 00:08:47,656
walk.

200
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:49,256
I couldn't make the picture work.

201
00:08:49,368 --> 00:08:50,780
The fish are close.

202
00:08:50,893 --> 00:08:52,206
So are the visitors.

203
00:08:52,318 --> 00:08:55,318
And this is where their families
have lived for generations.

204
00:08:55,430 --> 00:08:57,368
One more thing, said quietly.

205
00:08:57,481 --> 00:08:59,843
There's no room on the
island for a cemetery.

206
00:08:59,956 --> 00:09:01,905
People are buried on another island.

207
00:09:02,018 --> 00:09:04,356
Even that, they've found a way to carry.

208
00:09:06,076 --> 00:09:08,626
This morning I tried
packing the backpack again,

209
00:09:08,676 --> 00:09:10,426
to see if it had learned anything.

210
00:09:10,538 --> 00:09:11,338
It hadn't.

211
00:09:11,439 --> 00:09:13,088
It still smelled like the sea.

212
00:09:13,201 --> 00:09:15,751
I laid it on the drawing of
the island in my notebook,

213
00:09:15,801 --> 00:09:17,363
and it covered every house.

214
00:09:17,476 --> 00:09:18,276
NOPE.

215
00:09:18,288 --> 00:09:20,763
That stamp is about me, not the island.

216
00:09:20,876 --> 00:09:23,326
The people out there built
their own ground out of coral,

217
00:09:23,376 --> 00:09:24,214
by hand.

218
00:09:24,326 --> 00:09:26,988
They keep it going with
water that arrives by boat,

219
00:09:27,101 --> 00:09:28,576
and a sea that keeps knocking.

220
00:09:28,688 --> 00:09:32,288
A home there takes patience, a
good boat and very little luggage.

221
00:09:32,401 --> 00:09:35,113
I arrived with a mountain
of luggage and no boat.

222
00:09:35,226 --> 00:09:38,813
Honestly, I'd go back tomorrow for
one more evening in that square,

223
00:09:38,901 --> 00:09:41,326
carrying a bag the size of a lunchbox.

224
00:09:41,438 --> 00:09:45,138
If you travel with one small bag, love
the water and don't mind hearing your

225
00:09:45,188 --> 00:09:47,688
neighbour's radio,
you'd do better than me.

226
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:51,788
Somewhere out there, a mosquito
is still very pleased with itself.

227
00:09:51,901 --> 00:09:54,164
What would you leave on the
dock to fit on that island?

228
00:09:55,588 --> 00:09:58,101
The spray goes on the shelf, still sealed,

229
00:09:58,151 --> 00:10:01,388
next to the tunnel timetable
and the jar of almost no opals.

230
00:10:01,501 --> 00:10:03,251
The fish waves from the freezer.

231
00:10:03,363 --> 00:10:06,213
Next: an island very far from anywhere,

232
00:10:06,263 --> 00:10:09,351
where everyone once had to
leave, and then chose to go back.

233
00:10:09,463 --> 00:10:11,163
I'm taking the small backpack.
